@meta
id: 04-06
title: Let the gutter do the sum
chapter: 04
type: lesson
skills: count-prefix, relativenumber
par: 18
minutes: 5
vscode: nothing like it
open: orders.csv
vimrc: set relativenumber

@brief
Every counted jump so far has cost you a subtraction: you are on line 1,
you want line 27, so you work out 26. That tax disappears with one
setting.

`set relativenumber` prints, next to every line, how far that line is from
the cursor. Not its line number — its distance. The line you want says
`14` in the margin, so the jump is `14j`. No arithmetic, no reading of
line numbers, no counting. It has been switched on for this file.

The export is missing a currency on one row: `ORD-1023` has no `GBP` at
the end. Add `,GBP` so it matches every other row.

Move the cursor and watch the whole margin renumber itself around you.
That is the feature.

The row is far enough down that an arrow key means holding it and
watching. That is not a slower way of doing this; it is a different job,
in which you supervise a cursor instead of aiming one.

@keys
| `set relativenumber` | distances in the margin instead of line numbers |
| `23j` | down twenty-three lines |
| `A` | append at the end of the line |

@hints
Find the row with your eyes first — it is the one whose line is four
fields long instead of five. Then read the number the margin has already
put next to it.
--
Whatever number is printed beside that row, that is the count. Type it,
then `j`.
--
`23j`, then `A`, then `,GBP`, then `Esc` and `:wq`.

@solution
    23j A ,GBP <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Fourteen keystrokes and no mental arithmetic at all. You read `23` off
the screen and typed it.

Most vim users run with both: `set relativenumber number`, often written
`set rnu nu`, which shows distances everywhere except the cursor line,
where it shows the real line number. That gives you the number you need
for jumping and the number you need for talking about the file with other
people or a stack trace. Chapter 28 is where you will put it in a config
so you never think about it again.

A word of warning while it is new: the numbers move. They are distances
from wherever the cursor is right now, so the moment you jump, they are
all different. Read, jump, then read again. Do not read two numbers and
try to use them both.

